    "reason": "While '恭敬' (kyoukei) does mean respect or reverence, it carries a strong sense of deep veneration, often used in formal or literary contexts, or towards deities/superiors. It is significantly narrower and more formal than the general English verb 'respect'. It is not the most common or natural verb a general learner would use for 'respect' in everyday situations. More common and general verbs for 'to respect' are 尊敬する (sonkei suru) or 敬う (uyamau).",
